// Nouns describes each noun the methods' commands have.
func Nouns() []apicli.Noun {
	return []apicli.Noun{
		{Name: "alert-channels", Short: "List, test, create, update or delete alert channels", Long: "A channel is somewhere alerts are sent, such as a webhook. What signs in to it\nis sealed, never shown again, and a plan says only that it changed. Channels are\nresources: creating, changing and deleting one is a change, with a plan, that\nundo takes back. dockwell alert-channels test sends a test message and says what\nthe far end answered.\n\ndockwell alert-channels create, update and delete each propose a change, show\nits plan, and ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "alert-checks", Short: "List, create, update or delete alert checks", Long: "A check is an address Dockwell asks every so often whether it answers as it\nshould, from the control server: an uptime check. It reads down once as many\nruns in a row as it says have failed, which the check-down rule alerts on.\nChecks are resources: creating, changing and deleting one is a change, with a\nplan, that undo takes back. dockwell alert-checks list shows how each stands and\nits last runs.\n\ndockwell alert-checks create, update and delete each propose a change, show its\nplan, and ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "alert-deliveries", Short: "Return the newest messages to every channel", Long: "A delivery is a message Dockwell sent a channel, a test among them: what it was\nabout, when, and what the far end answered, with nothing that signs in. Each\nchannel's last one says whether it works."},
		{Name: "alert-mutes", Short: "List the mutes in force", Long: "A mute holds what a rule or a server sends for a while, during an outage, with\nno change to apply. dockwell alerts mute starts one and dockwell alerts unmute\nends it; both go on Activity."},
		{Name: "alert-rules", Short: "List, create, update or delete alert rules", Long: "A rule is something Dockwell watches for, such as a disk above 90% for 5\nminutes, and who it tells. An install starts with a rule for each condition,\nwhich sends to every channel once there is one. Rules are resources: creating,\nchanging and deleting one is a change, with a plan, that undo takes back.\n\ndockwell alert-rules create, update and delete each propose a change, show its\nplan, and ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "alerts", Short: "List, mute, acknowledge, unacknowledge or unmute alerts", Long: "An alert is a rule holding for one thing, such as a server's disk, from when it\nfires until it resolves. Dockwell evaluates the rules on the control plane, from\nwhat it hears of servers already, and sends each alert to the rule's channels,\nand again when it resolves. dockwell alerts list shows what's firing and what\nwas. dockwell alerts mute holds what a rule or a server sends for a while, with\nno change to apply, and goes on Activity."},
		{Name: "api-tokens", Short: "Create, list or revoke API tokens", Long: "TokenService manages API tokens: secrets for scripts, the CLI and integrations,\neach acting for the person who made it, within a scope. See\ndocs/adr/0016-scoped-api-tokens.md.\n\nSend a token in the Authorization header, as \"Bearer dwt_…\", with REST, Connect,\ngRPC or gRPC-Web alike. A read-only token may call the methods whose effect is\nEFFECT_READ; a read-write token may also call EFFECT_PLAN and EFFECT_APPLY\nmethods. No token may call an EFFECT_DESTRUCTIVE method, AuthService, or this\nservice: they need a signed-in session."},
		{Name: "audit-events", Short: "Return the audit log", Long: "AuditService reads the audit log: every event Dockwell records of who did what,\nfrom where, as it was recorded. That includes what the timeline leaves out:\nrefusals, such as a wrong code or a refused token, a token's first use from an\naddress, signing out, and the install's own setup. See\ndocs/adr/0047-the-audit-log-for-a-person.md.\n\nIts events carry the words Dockwell wrote for people, which name the addresses\npeople signed in from and were refused from, so only a person signed in reads\nit, never an API token."},
		{Name: "auth-state", Short: "Say whether the install has an owner", Long: "The install's auth state says whether it has an owner yet, who is signed in on a\nrequest, and which address passkeys are made for there. dockwell auth-state get\nneeds no sign-in."},
		{Name: "backups", Short: "List, get, preview, create, delete, policies, destinations, fetch or restore backups, and 1 more", Long: "BackupService is an app's volumes and databases, backed up on the server that\nhas them. Taking a backup is an event: it changes no desired state, so it makes\nno revision, and a failed one shows on the timeline. Restoring one is a change\nwith a plan, which says what it replaces and what stops, and which only a person\ncan apply. See docs/adr/0033-backups-of-volumes-and-databases.md.\n\nA backup holds what its app stored, secrets among them, so nothing here gives\nanyone its bytes."},
		{Name: "catalog", Short: "List, get, installs, upgrade, install or sources catalog, and 3 more", Long: "CatalogService lists the apps you can install, and plans installing one as a\nproject of its own: a Compose file with sensible ports and generated passwords,\nsealed, through the same plan as any other change. Nothing runs until you apply\nit. See docs/adr/0029-a-catalog-format-fed-from-other-catalogs.md."},
		{Name: "changes", Short: "Propose, get, list, apply, watch, undo, confirm or discard changes", Long: "A change is a set of edits to managed state, with the plan that carries them\nout. You propose edits and get a plan back; applying it writes immutable\nrevisions and runs its steps on the servers; undoing it makes a new plan that\nputs things back. A change applies only as it was planned, and a plan never\nshows a secret, only that it changed.\n\nSee docs/adr/0009-resources-revisions-and-plans.md and\ndocs/adr/0017-the-change-api-and-the-agent-stream.md."},
		{Name: "claim-link", Short: "Check, begin or finish claim link", Long: "A claim link, which dockwell run and dockwell claim print, creates the install's\nowner with a passkey, and works once. dockwell claim-link check describes the\nserver a link claims, and dockwell claim-link begin and dockwell claim-link\nfinish make the passkey, which takes a browser: open the link in one."},
		{Name: "compose", Short: "Import, adopt, check or export compose", Long: "ComposeService turns a Docker Compose file into a project's services, volumes\nand networks, and any of them back into one. Each file gets a project of its\nown, so two files with a service of one name run side by side, on networks of\ntheir own, as they do under docker compose. An environment is one Compose\nproject: its containers carry the labels Docker Compose gives its own, so docker\ncompose sees them, and an exported file runs the same with docker compose alone\n(PRD principle 6). See docs/adr/0021-the-docker-driver.md and\ndocs/adr/0026-the-app-screens.md."},
		{Name: "containers", Short: "List, adopt, create, update or delete containers", Long: "ContainerService reads the containers on a server that Dockwell didn't make:\nthose that were there before it, and those someone runs by hand or with a\nCompose file of their own. Reading them changes nothing, and nothing here comes\nfrom a container's environment, which may hold secrets, or its labels and\ncommand, which may too. See docs/adr/0031-containers-dockwell-didnt-make.md.\n\nA service's containers aren't here: they are the service's. dockwell logs read\nand dockwell logs follow read what one of these prints, given its server.\n\ndockwell containers create, update and delete each propose a change, show its\nplan, and ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "control-plane", Short: "Return what serves the panel and API", Long: "The control plane serves the panel and API, beside an agent of its own. It says\nthe build it runs, where it keeps its state and the install's key, the addresses\nit answers on, and how it's doing. Most of that comes from how dockwell run was\nstarted, and changes there."},
		{Name: "databases", Short: "List, get, add user or change password databases", Long: "DatabaseService is the services that run a database Dockwell knows: PostgreSQL,\nMySQL, MariaDB, Redis and Valkey, from their official images, whether Dockwell\nmade them or took them on. A database is a service, so it changes as any service\ndoes, by a change with a plan. What this adds is what the database itself says,\nread in its own container with its own tools, which sign in with what its\nenvironment already holds, so no password passes through Dockwell. See\ndocs/adr/0038-databases.md.\n\nNothing here carries a password, nor a value of a database's environment."},
		{Name: "domains", Short: "List or check domains", Long: "DomainService says where the domains of your routes stand: what each sends\nwhere, and whether its certificate is pending, issued or failed, with what to do\nwhen it failed. Change routes with ChangeService, as kind \"route\". See\ndocs/adr/0024-the-caddy-ingress-driver.md."},
		{Name: "drift", Short: "List, revert or adopt drift", Long: "Drift is a difference between what a server has and the latest revision of a\nresource it should have, as when someone changed it by hand. dockwell drift list\nasks each connected server's agent, and changes nothing. dockwell drift revert\nplans putting the revision back, and dockwell drift adopt plans keeping what the\nserver has as a new revision: each is a change, with a plan to apply."},
		{Name: "events", Short: "List or watch events", Long: "EventService is the timeline: every change to managed state, with who made it\nand from where, beside what servers and people did that belongs with it, such as\na server joining or someone signing in. See docs/adr/0020-the-event-timeline.md.\n\nA change is one event, which moves on as the change does: planned, then\napplying, then applied or failed. Rollbacks and undos are changes of their own,\neach naming the change it reverts. A discarded change isn't on the timeline, and\nnor is the change that set up an install's default project and environment.\nEvery other event comes from the audit log, as it was recorded.\n\nEvents carry no secret, no spec and no address.\n\ndockwell events watch streams, so it has no REST path. A REST client polls GET\n/v1/events, or opens the stream with Connect, gRPC or gRPC-Web, with the same\ncredentials."},
		{Name: "files", Short: "List, read, save, make, move, delete, restore, purge, upload or download files, and 4 more", Long: "FileService browses, reads, saves, makes, moves and deletes the files on a\nserver, through its agent. Files on a server aren't managed state, so nothing\nhere is a revision or has a plan. A save can't be undone, and a delete can for 7\ndays where it's on the same filesystem as Dockwell's data. See\ndocs/adr/0032-files-on-a-server.md and\ndocs/adr/0045-a-file-manager-on-a-server.md.\n\nEvery method needs a signed-in session: no API token can call them, whatever its\nscope. The agent refuses what holds Dockwell's keys, secrets' values and the\nserver's own credentials for everyone, with the reason \"file_refused\":\ndocs/security/files.md lists them."},
		{Name: "firewall", Short: "Reads", Long: "FirewallService says what filters a server's inbound traffic. Reading it changes\nnothing. See docs/adr/0052-a-servers-firewall-ssh-and-fail2ban.md."},
		{Name: "health", Short: "Return how each service that exists now is", Long: "HealthService says how services are, as far as anything checked, from what each\nserver's agent last said. It never claims health nobody checked: a service with\nno check is HEALTH_STATE_NO_CHECK, and a server whose agent isn't connected is\nHEALTH_STATE_UNKNOWN, with what it last said marked stale. See\ndocs/adr/0027-health-checks-rollouts-and-rollback.md.\n\nReading it needs the read scope alone, as what a check printed is cut short and\nhas what the container's environment holds masked. It isn't a log."},
		{Name: "images", Short: "Search, tags or inspect images", Long: "ImageService reads images from their registries for a form, through the server\nan app will run on, so a form can offer names and tags, say whether an image is\nthere as it's typed, and fill in what an image declares. Nothing is pulled, and\nnothing changes. Each read goes to that server's agent, which reads with the\npull's network and mirrors, and keeps what it read for a while; the control\nplane keeps none of it. An answer says nothing of the server, such as which\nimages it has, which is the install's (ADR 0048). See\ndocs/adr/0062-reading-an-image-from-its-registry.md."},
		{Name: "import", Short: "Read what 1Panel keeps on a server", Long: "ImportService moves what another panel runs on a server into Dockwell: what can\nrun as it is is taken on with no restart, what can't is made again, and the rest\nis left with why. Each import is a plan a person applies. See\ndocs/adr/0056-importing-a-1panel-server.md."},
		{Name: "invite", Short: "Check, begin or finish invite", Long: "An invite's link, which an admin or the owner makes in the panel's Members,\nsigns in a new person with the role and the place it names, and works once.\ndockwell invite check says who invited them, as what and where, and dockwell\ninvite begin and dockwell invite finish make their passkey, which takes a\nbrowser at the panel's domain: open the link in one."},
		{Name: "invites", Short: "Create or revoke invites", Long: "An invite asks someone, by email, to sign in with a role in a place, the install\nor a project. An admin or the owner makes it in the panel, with a passkey, and\nsends the link it shows once: Dockwell sends no mail. Making and revoking one\nneeds a signed-in session, which no API token gives."},
		{Name: "join-tickets", Short: "Create or get join tickets", Long: "A join ticket adds one server: dockwell join with it, on the new server, gives\nthat server a name and a certificate, and its agent connects. dockwell\njoin-tickets create makes one with a passkey, and dockwell join-tickets get says\nwhether a server has used it. dockwell token create makes the same ticket on the\ncontrol server."},
		{Name: "logs", Short: "Sources, read or follow logs", Long: "LogService reads what a service's containers print: live, or from what each\nserver still has. See docs/adr/0025-logs-per-deploy.md.\n\nEach server's agent reads Docker's logs as they are asked for, and log lines\nnever sit in the control plane's store. A server keeps what Docker keeps for a\nrunning or stopped container, and the logs of the last failed and the last\nreplaced container of each service, within a disk budget.\n\nLogs often hold secrets and personal data that apps print, and Dockwell doesn't\nredact them. So an API token reads them only with the grant TOKEN_GRANT_LOGS,\nand reading them is on record in the audit log.\n\ndockwell logs follow streams, so it has no REST path. A REST client pages\nthrough dockwell logs read, or opens the stream with Connect, gRPC or gRPC-Web,\nwith the same credentials."},
		{Name: "members", Short: "Return everyone who can sign in", Long: "Members are the people who sign in to the install, with their roles: on the\nwhole install, or in projects. Listing them names people and their emails, so it\nneeds a signed-in session as an admin or the owner, which no API token gives."},
		{Name: "passkeys", Short: "List, begin add, finish add or remove passkeys", Long: "Passkeys are how you sign in to the panel. A browser, a phone or a security key\nmakes each one, for one address, and only it can use it. Adding and removing\nthem needs a signed-in session, which no API token gives, and a passkey you\nalready have to confirm it, so you do it in the panel, under Profile and\nsecurity."},
		{Name: "processes", Short: "Watch or end processes", Long: "ProcessService shows what runs on a server, process by process, and ends one. A\nserver's agent reads its processes only while someone watches them. See\ndocs/adr/0041-processes-on-a-server.md.\n\nEvery method needs a signed-in session: no API token can call them, whatever its\nscope, as a process's command line can hold what was typed with it, a password\nincluded, and ending one is as much as root on the server."},
		{Name: "resources", Short: "Return the resources that exist now", Long: "ResourceService reads the resources Dockwell manages: projects, environments,\nservices, volumes, networks, routes, containers and firewall rules. Change them\nwith ChangeService.\n\nIt names resources and says where they live, and never carries a spec, which may\nhold secrets. A spec's display_name, which never does, is the one exception."},
		{Name: "secrets", Short: "Reveal, create, update or delete secrets", Long: "A secret is a value of an environment that its services use without anyone\nreading it back, such as a database password. Dockwell keeps it encrypted, sends\nit only to the servers that run a service that uses it, and never shows it in\nplans, history or logs. Its value is read from standard input or a file, never\nfrom a flag. Revealing it takes a passkey, in the panel, and is audited: no API\ntoken can.\n\ndockwell secrets create, update and delete each propose a change, show its plan,\nand ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "servers", Short: "List, get or watch servers, and 3 more", Long: "The servers Dockwell manages, with their live state as each server's agent\nreports it. dockwell servers watch has no REST path: REST has no way to stream\nmessages, so a REST client polls dockwell servers list or dockwell servers get\ninstead, and anything that wants the stream opens it with Connect, gRPC or\ngRPC-Web. See docs/adr/0015-rest-and-openapi-from-the-schema.md."},
		{Name: "services", Short: "Restart, list, revisions, deploy, rollback, open, create, update or delete services, and 1 more", Long: "A service is anything that runs in an environment, placed on servers. dockwell\nservices restart plans a restart, which is a change with a plan: it records no\nrevision, as a restart changes no spec, and nothing to undo.\n\nSee docs/adr/0027-health-checks-rollouts-and-rollback.md.\n\ndockwell services create, update and delete each propose a change, show its\nplan, and ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "sessions", Short: "List or revoke sessions, and 8 more", Long: "A session is a browser you're signed in on. Signing in takes a passkey, a code\nfrom an authenticator app, a recovery code, or a one-time link from dockwell\nrecover, and sets a session cookie. No API token can sign in, or list or end\nsessions: how you sign in changes only by signing in, in the panel."},
		{Name: "settings", Short: "Get, set name or set time zone settings", Long: "The install's settings are what it's called, the time zone new schedules start\nin, and how its panel is served. They change what Dockwell says and suggests,\nnot what runs, so a change needs no plan, and each is on the activity log with\nwhat it replaced."},
		{Name: "system", Short: "Return the build of the control plane that answers", Long: "SystemService describes the Dockwell install itself. It needs no sign-in, so the\nCLI, agents and other tools can check what they are talking to first."},
		{Name: "tasks", Short: "List, get, runs, run, create, update or delete tasks", Long: "TaskService is work that runs on a schedule: a shell command on a server, a\ncommand in an app's image, or an HTTP request. A task is a resource of kind\n\"task\", so making, changing, pausing and deleting one is a change with a plan,\nthrough ChangeService. Each server's agent keeps its own tasks' time, so they\nrun while the control plane is away, and the control plane keeps what each run\ndid. See docs/adr/0053-scheduled-tasks.md.\n\ndockwell tasks create, update and delete each propose a change, show its plan,\nand ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "terminals", Short: "Open, list, attach, write or close terminals", Long: "TerminalService opens a shell on a server, through its agent, and carries what's\ntyped and what it prints. A server's shell is root's, so it's as good as root:\nsee docs/adr/0037-a-terminal-on-a-server.md and docs/security/terminal.md.\n\nEvery method needs an admin in a signed-in session: no API token can call them,\nwhatever its scope. A terminal belongs to the person and the session that opened\nit, and nobody else can reach it. Opening one takes a passkey, just now. Opening\nand closing one are on record in Activity, and what's typed and printed is never\nrecorded.\n\nA terminal runs on its server apart from any connection, so a client that loses\nits stream attaches again from the last byte it had, and misses nothing that\nfits in what the server keeps. dockwell terminals attach streams, so it has no\nREST path."},
		{Name: "totp", Short: "Get, disable or unlock TOTP, and 4 more", Long: "Code sign-in lets you sign in with a code from an authenticator app, or with a\none-time recovery code, beside your passkeys. Wrong codes slow it down, and too\nmany lock it until dockwell totp unlock turns it back on. Setting it up needs a\nsigned-in session, which no API token gives, and your passkey to confirm it, so\nyou do it in the panel, under Profile and security."},
		{Name: "updates", Short: "Get or check updates", Long: "UpdateService says which package updates wait on a server, through its agent,\nwhich reads them with the server's own package manager: apt, dnf or apk. Nothing\nis read while nobody asks, and a server's package lists are refreshed at most\ndaily. See docs/adr/0043-package-updates-on-a-server.md."},
		{Name: "variables", Short: "List, create, update or delete variables", Long: "A variable is a value of an environment that its services use, such as\nLOG_LEVEL=info. It isn't secret: plans and the panel show it. Services use\nvariables and secrets by reference, under the names they expect, so changing one\nis a plan that restarts every service that uses it.\n\ndockwell variables create, update and delete each propose a change, show its\nplan, and ask before applying it."},
		{Name: "websites", Short: "List, runtimes, preview, create, update or delete websites", Long: "WebsiteService lists your websites, and plans making one, changing what it\nserves and deleting it, each as a change to apply or discard. A website is a\nproject of its own: Dockwell writes its route, and the service that serves it,\nfrom what you choose, such as a URL to send its requests to. Its domain gets its\ncertificate as any route's does, or none behind a tunnel or a proxy that ends\nTLS. See docs/adr/0039-websites-as-projects-of-their-own.md."},
	}
}

// Kinds describes the commands for each kind of resource an edit's spec
// can hold.
func Kinds() []*apicli.Kind {
	return []*apicli.Kind{
		{
			Field: "project",
			Noun:  "projects",
			Short: "A project, kind \"project\"",
			Long:  "Project is a product or client in the organization. It holds environments, and\nscopes members, secrets and domains. An install starts with one, named\n\"default\", which stays out of sight until there is a second. Deleting one takes\nits environments with it, once nothing lives in them.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "display_name", Help: "How the panel shows it, such as \"Acme web\". Empty shows its name, which URLs and the CLI use."},
				{Path: "catalog.entry", Help: "The entry's ID, such as \"postgres\"."},
				{Path: "catalog.version", Help: "The app's version as installed, such as \"17.11\"."},
				{Path: "catalog.release", Help: "The entry's release as installed, which a newer one's goes past."},
				{Path: "catalog.repository", Help: "The repository the entry came from, as the control plane knows it: Dockwell's own for its built-in catalog, or a catalog source's. Never what the entry says."},
				{Path: "catalog.commit", Help: "The commit it came from: the build's, or the source's when it was installed."},
				{Path: "catalog.values", Help: "What each input that isn't a secret was given, by name, so an upgrade gives the same."},
				{Path: "catalog.file", Help: "The entry's Compose file at this release, with its ${NAME}s as written, so it holds no secret. Read with values, it's what the file gave, which an upgrade needs to tell that from what a person changed since. Empty for an install from before Dockwell kept it, which can't be upgraded."},
				{Path: "catalog.catalog", Help: "The catalog source it came from, by name, or empty for Dockwell's own. An upgrade comes only from the same catalog, at the same repository and folder, whatever else has an entry of its ID (ADR 0055)."},
				{Path: "catalog.path", Help: "The folder in the repository its source reads, for an entry from a source."},
				{Path: "website.proxy.url", Help: "Where to send them, such as \"https://backend.example.com\" or \"http://10.0.0.5:8080/app\": http or https, a host, and a port and a path if it needs them, with no query. A path goes in front of each request's own. It's reached from a container, as any app reaches the internet, so it can't be the server's own loopback: give port for that."},
				{Path: "website.proxy.port", Help: "A port on the server itself, such as 3000, in place of url. Whatever listens there sees every visitor as local, so a person approves it."},
				{Path: "website.proxy.send_target_host", Help: "Sends the url's own host as the Host header, in place of the website's domain, as a target that serves several names needs, such as another site over HTTPS. For a url only."},
				{Path: "website.folder.root", Help: "The folder, as an absolute path on the server, such as \"/var/www/blog\". Its web server reads it as nobody's user, so what it serves must be readable by everyone. Docker makes a folder that isn't there, empty and owned by root."},
				{Path: "website.folder.index", Help: "The files a request for a folder gets, the first there is, such as \"index.html\". Empty takes index.html, then index.htm."},
				{Path: "website.folder.single_page_app", Help: "Answers a path with no file of its own with the index at the folder's top, as an app that routes in the browser needs, rather than with a 404."},
				{Path: "website.folder.owner", Help: "Who owns the folder Dockwell makes when it isn't there, as \"uid:gid\", such as \"1000:1000\", so you can put the site's files in it as yourself. Empty leaves it to Docker, which makes it root's. A folder that's there keeps its owner."},
				{Path: "website.php.root", Help: "The folder, as an absolute path on the server, such as \"/var/www/shop\"."},
				{Path: "website.php.version", Help: "The PHP version, such as \"8.3\": one Dockwell offers, as ListWebsiteRuntimes says. Empty takes the newest."},
				{Path: "website.php.index", Help: "The files a request for a folder gets, the first there is. Empty takes index.php, then index.html."},
				{Path: "website.php.files_only", Help: "Answers a path with no file of its own with a 404, rather than sending it to the folder's index.php, as WordPress, Laravel and most PHP apps want."},
				{Path: "website.php.upload_limit_mb", Help: "The most a request may send, in MiB, as PHP's M counts, such as an upload: what PHP takes and what the web server lets through. 0 takes 64."},
				{Path: "website.php.user", Help: "Who PHP runs as, as \"uid:gid\", such as \"1000:1000\": the folder's owner, so PHP can write in it, and what it writes, such as uploads, keeps its owner. A number alone is taken for both. Empty is www-data, \"82:82\". Never root's, nor root's group. PHP-FPM's master runs as it too. A folder that isn't there is made as its."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "environment",
			Noun:  "environments",
			Short: "An environment, kind \"environment\"",
			Long:  "Environment is a stage of a project, such as production or staging, with its own\nconfig. A project starts with one, named \"production\", which stays out of sight\nuntil there is a second.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "display_name", Help: "How the panel shows it, such as \"Staging\". Empty shows its name."},
				{Path: "protection", Help: "Whether a developer's change here waits for an admin or the owner. Every environment Dockwell makes has it written: protected for one named production, open for any other. An edit that leaves it out keeps what's there. Unset, as on an environment from before, it reads as that default by its name (ADR 0049). Making one with other than the default, or changing it, is an admin's."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "service",
			Noun:  "services",
			Short: "A service, kind \"service\"",
			Long:  "Service is anything that runs in an environment, placed on servers: for now an\napp from an image, run as one container on each server it is placed on, and two\nwhile a deploy rolls, with the labels Docker Compose gives a service's\ncontainer, so docker compose sees it. A Compose file becomes services, volumes\nand networks. Websites, databases, catalog apps and jobs come later. Where it\nruns isn't part of the spec: each revision is deployed on its servers.\n\nA service uses its environment's variables and secrets by reference: the\nenvironment holds each value once, and a service names the ones it uses, under\nthe names it expects. So changing a value changes every service that uses it, in\none plan.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "app.image", Help: "The image to run, such as \"ghcr.io/acme/web:1.4.2\"."},
				{Path: "app.command", Help: "Overrides the image's command when set."},
				{Path: "app.env", Help: "Environment variables, which may hold secrets.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "app.ports", Help: "Ports published on the server."},
				{Path: "app.restart", Help: "What Docker does when the container stops."},
				{Path: "app.labels", Help: "Labels on the container, for tools that read them. Keys that start with \"com.docker.compose.\" or \"dev.dockwell.\" are Dockwell's own."},
				{Path: "app.entrypoint", Help: "Overrides the image's entrypoint when set. Empty takes the image's; no_entrypoint runs none."},
				{Path: "app.mounts", Help: "Volumes and server paths mounted into the container."},
				{Path: "app.networks", Help: "The networks the container joins, by name. A service's are networks in its environment; a container's on its own are Docker's. Empty joins Docker's default bridge network."},
				{Path: "app.user", Help: "The user the command runs as, such as \"1000\" or \"node\". Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "app.working_dir", Help: "The directory the command runs in. Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.test", Help: "A command, as Docker takes it: [\"CMD\", \"curl\", \"-f\", \"http://localhost\"], [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"curl -f http://localhost || exit 1\"], or [\"NONE\"] to turn off the image's check."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.interval", Help: "How often it runs, such as \"30s\". Empty takes Docker's default for a command, 30 s, and 10 s for http and tcp. At least 1 s for http and tcp."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.timeout", Help: "How long one check may take, such as \"5s\". Empty takes Docker's default for a command, 30 s, and 5 s for http and tcp. At most 1 min for http and tcp."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.start_period", Help: "How long the container has to start before failures count, such as \"1m\". Empty takes none."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.retries", Help: "How many failures in a row make it unhealthy. 0 takes 3."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.http.port", Help: "The port the app listens on inside its container, such as 8080."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.http.path", Help: "The path to ask for, such as \"/healthz\". Empty asks for \"/\"."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.http.host", Help: "The Host header to send, for an app that refuses one it doesn't know, such as \"app.example.com\". Empty sends the container's address."},
				{Path: "app.healthcheck.tcp.port", Help: "The port the app listens on inside its container, such as 5432."},
				{Path: "app.no_entrypoint", Help: "Runs no entrypoint, not even the image's, as Compose's \"entrypoint: []\" does: the command runs on its own. Takes no entrypoint beside it."},
				{Path: "app.stop_grace_period", Help: "How long the container has to stop before Docker kills it, in whole seconds, such as \"30s\", as Compose's stop_grace_period. Empty takes Docker's default of 10 s."},
				{Path: "app.rollout.order", Help: "Which comes first, the new container or stopping the old one. Unset takes the safest there is for what the service publishes and mounts: the new one first, unless both would publish one server port, or a volume, a server path or the data its image keeps would be written to by two containers at once."},
				{Path: "app.rollout.drain", Help: "How long what is in flight to the old container has to finish once no new request goes to it, such as \"1m\". Empty takes 30 s. What outlasts it, such as a long-lived WebSocket, ends with the container."},
				{Path: "app.external_networks", Help: "Networks a service joins that Dockwell didn't make, by their names in Docker, such as \"1panel-network\". Each must be on the server already: Dockwell never makes, changes or deletes one. Whatever else is on it can reach the service, so joining one is host access, which a person approves. A container on its own names Docker's networks in networks."},
				{Path: "app.host_network", Help: "Runs a service on the server's own network, with none of its own, as Compose's \"network_mode: host\" does. It is host access, and takes no networks, ports or aliases. A container on its own names \"host\" in networks."},
				{Path: "app.aliases", Help: "Other names containers on a service's networks reach it by, beside its service's name, such as a Compose file's container_name. No two services on a network can answer to one name."},
				{Path: "app.resources.cpus", Help: "The most CPUs it may use, such as \"0.5\" or \"2\", to three decimal places. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "app.resources.memory", Help: "The most memory it may use, such as \"512M\" or \"2G\", from 6M, in units of 1024. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "app.resources.memory_reservation", Help: "The memory Docker tries to leave it when the server runs short, such as \"256M\", from 6M and no more than memory. Empty reserves none."},
				{Path: "app.cap_add", Help: "Linux capabilities it gets beyond Docker's default ones, such as \"NET_ADMIN\", or \"ALL\". It is host access."},
				{Path: "app.cap_drop", Help: "Linux capabilities it gives up, such as \"NET_RAW\", or \"ALL\"."},
				{Path: "app.group_add", Help: "Groups its command joins beside its user's, by name or number, such as \"audio\" or \"999\". It is host access: joining the group that owns Docker's socket is the same as root."},
				{Path: "app.privileged", Help: "Runs it with every device and capability on the server, the same as root. It is host access."},
				{Path: "app.restart_retries", Help: "How many times on-failure restarts it before giving up. 0 has no limit. Only on-failure takes one."},
				{Path: "app.references", Help: "Which of \"command\", \"entrypoint\" and \"healthcheck.test\" name values rather than hold them. In a field listed here, ${NAME} is the value of NAME in the container's environment, or of what its service uses under that name, and $$ is a $ of its own, as in a Compose file. So a plan, an export and what an agent reports show the name, a rotation reaches it, and the value is written in only as the container is made. In any other field, a $ is a $."},
				{Path: "app.shm_size", Help: "The size of its /dev/shm, such as \"1G\", in units of 1024, as Compose's shm_size. What it holds counts against resources.memory. Empty takes Docker's default of 64M, which is too little for Chromium on a heavy page."},
				{Path: "app.tmpfs", Help: "Filesystems held in memory at paths in the container. What is in one goes when the container does, so a deploy starts it empty."},
				{Path: "app.expose", Help: "Ports it exposes to what shares a network with it, beyond what its image exposes and what it publishes, such as \"3000/tcp\". It opens nothing on the server. What finds containers through Docker reads it, as Traefik does for the port to reach."},
				{Path: "app.extra_hosts", Help: "Names it reaches at an address of its own choosing, as hostname:address, such as \"host.docker.internal:host-gateway\", where host-gateway is the server as its containers reach it. It adds to the container's /etc/hosts, and reaches nothing a container couldn't without it. Numbered in the catalog's block, 80 to 89, apart from fields other work adds."},
				{Path: "variables", Help: "The environment's variables the service uses, each under the name the service sees it as: DATABASE_HOST=DB_HOST gives the service the variable DB_HOST as DATABASE_HOST. An empty value means the variable of the same name."},
				{Path: "secrets", Help: "The environment's secrets the service uses, named as for variables. Using a secret doesn't let anyone read it: its value goes only to the servers the service runs on, and only their agents decrypt it."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "container",
			Noun:  "containers",
			Short: "A container run by hand on a server, kind \"container\"",
			Long:  "Container is one Docker container: run by hand on a server, or a service's app.\nDocker fills in from the image what a container leaves out, such as its command\nor working directory, and that isn't drift.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "image", Help: "The image to run, such as \"ghcr.io/acme/web:1.4.2\"."},
				{Path: "command", Help: "Overrides the image's command when set."},
				{Path: "env", Help: "Environment variables, which may hold secrets.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "ports", Help: "Ports published on the server."},
				{Path: "restart", Help: "What Docker does when the container stops."},
				{Path: "labels", Help: "Labels on the container, for tools that read them. Keys that start with \"com.docker.compose.\" or \"dev.dockwell.\" are Dockwell's own."},
				{Path: "entrypoint", Help: "Overrides the image's entrypoint when set. Empty takes the image's; no_entrypoint runs none."},
				{Path: "mounts", Help: "Volumes and server paths mounted into the container."},
				{Path: "networks", Help: "The networks the container joins, by name. A service's are networks in its environment; a container's on its own are Docker's. Empty joins Docker's default bridge network."},
				{Path: "user", Help: "The user the command runs as, such as \"1000\" or \"node\". Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "working_dir", Help: "The directory the command runs in. Empty takes the image's."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.test", Help: "A command, as Docker takes it: [\"CMD\", \"curl\", \"-f\", \"http://localhost\"], [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"curl -f http://localhost || exit 1\"], or [\"NONE\"] to turn off the image's check."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.interval", Help: "How often it runs, such as \"30s\". Empty takes Docker's default for a command, 30 s, and 10 s for http and tcp. At least 1 s for http and tcp."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.timeout", Help: "How long one check may take, such as \"5s\". Empty takes Docker's default for a command, 30 s, and 5 s for http and tcp. At most 1 min for http and tcp."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.start_period", Help: "How long the container has to start before failures count, such as \"1m\". Empty takes none."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.retries", Help: "How many failures in a row make it unhealthy. 0 takes 3."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.http.port", Help: "The port the app listens on inside its container, such as 8080."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.http.path", Help: "The path to ask for, such as \"/healthz\". Empty asks for \"/\"."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.http.host", Help: "The Host header to send, for an app that refuses one it doesn't know, such as \"app.example.com\". Empty sends the container's address."},
				{Path: "healthcheck.tcp.port", Help: "The port the app listens on inside its container, such as 5432."},
				{Path: "no_entrypoint", Help: "Runs no entrypoint, not even the image's, as Compose's \"entrypoint: []\" does: the command runs on its own. Takes no entrypoint beside it."},
				{Path: "stop_grace_period", Help: "How long the container has to stop before Docker kills it, in whole seconds, such as \"30s\", as Compose's stop_grace_period. Empty takes Docker's default of 10 s."},
				{Path: "rollout.order", Help: "Which comes first, the new container or stopping the old one. Unset takes the safest there is for what the service publishes and mounts: the new one first, unless both would publish one server port, or a volume, a server path or the data its image keeps would be written to by two containers at once."},
				{Path: "rollout.drain", Help: "How long what is in flight to the old container has to finish once no new request goes to it, such as \"1m\". Empty takes 30 s. What outlasts it, such as a long-lived WebSocket, ends with the container."},
				{Path: "external_networks", Help: "Networks a service joins that Dockwell didn't make, by their names in Docker, such as \"1panel-network\". Each must be on the server already: Dockwell never makes, changes or deletes one. Whatever else is on it can reach the service, so joining one is host access, which a person approves. A container on its own names Docker's networks in networks."},
				{Path: "host_network", Help: "Runs a service on the server's own network, with none of its own, as Compose's \"network_mode: host\" does. It is host access, and takes no networks, ports or aliases. A container on its own names \"host\" in networks."},
				{Path: "aliases", Help: "Other names containers on a service's networks reach it by, beside its service's name, such as a Compose file's container_name. No two services on a network can answer to one name."},
				{Path: "resources.cpus", Help: "The most CPUs it may use, such as \"0.5\" or \"2\", to three decimal places. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "resources.memory", Help: "The most memory it may use, such as \"512M\" or \"2G\", from 6M, in units of 1024. Empty has no limit."},
				{Path: "resources.memory_reservation", Help: "The memory Docker tries to leave it when the server runs short, such as \"256M\", from 6M and no more than memory. Empty reserves none."},
				{Path: "cap_add", Help: "Linux capabilities it gets beyond Docker's default ones, such as \"NET_ADMIN\", or \"ALL\". It is host access."},
				{Path: "cap_drop", Help: "Linux capabilities it gives up, such as \"NET_RAW\", or \"ALL\"."},
				{Path: "group_add", Help: "Groups its command joins beside its user's, by name or number, such as \"audio\" or \"999\". It is host access: joining the group that owns Docker's socket is the same as root."},
				{Path: "privileged", Help: "Runs it with every device and capability on the server, the same as root. It is host access."},
				{Path: "restart_retries", Help: "How many times on-failure restarts it before giving up. 0 has no limit. Only on-failure takes one."},
				{Path: "references", Help: "Which of \"command\", \"entrypoint\" and \"healthcheck.test\" name values rather than hold them. In a field listed here, ${NAME} is the value of NAME in the container's environment, or of what its service uses under that name, and $$ is a $ of its own, as in a Compose file. So a plan, an export and what an agent reports show the name, a rotation reaches it, and the value is written in only as the container is made. In any other field, a $ is a $."},
				{Path: "shm_size", Help: "The size of its /dev/shm, such as \"1G\", in units of 1024, as Compose's shm_size. What it holds counts against resources.memory. Empty takes Docker's default of 64M, which is too little for Chromium on a heavy page."},
				{Path: "tmpfs", Help: "Filesystems held in memory at paths in the container. What is in one goes when the container does, so a deploy starts it empty."},
				{Path: "expose", Help: "Ports it exposes to what shares a network with it, beyond what its image exposes and what it publishes, such as \"3000/tcp\". It opens nothing on the server. What finds containers through Docker reads it, as Traefik does for the port to reach."},
				{Path: "extra_hosts", Help: "Names it reaches at an address of its own choosing, as hostname:address, such as \"host.docker.internal:host-gateway\", where host-gateway is the server as its containers reach it. It adds to the container's /etc/hosts, and reaches nothing a container couldn't without it. Numbered in the catalog's block, 80 to 89, apart from fields other work adds."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "firewall_rule",
			Noun:  "firewall-rules",
			Short: "A firewall rule on a server, kind \"firewall.rule\"",
			Long:  "FirewallRule allows or blocks inbound traffic to a port on a server.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "action", Help: "Allow or block."},
				{Path: "protocol", Help: "TCP unless set."},
				{Path: "port", Help: "The port the rule covers."},
				{Path: "sources", Help: "Source addresses in CIDR form, such as \"203.0.113.0/24\". Empty means anywhere."},
				{Path: "comment", Help: "Why the rule exists, shown next to it."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "firewall_policy",
			Noun:  "firewall-policies",
			Short: "What a server's firewall does with what no rule lets in, kind \"firewall.policy\"",
			Long:  "FirewallPolicy is what a server's firewall does with inbound traffic no rule\nlets in, kind \"firewall.policy\", named \"default\", one for each server. Without\none, or with deny_everything_else off, what no rule drops comes in, as it did\nbefore Dockwell (ADR 0052).",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "deny_everything_else", Help: "Drop inbound traffic no rule lets in. What keeps the server reachable is still let in: connections already open, SSH from where people come in now, and on the control server its own ports."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "volume",
			Noun:  "volumes",
			Short: "A volume in an environment, kind \"volume\"",
			Long:  "Volume is a Docker volume in an environment, which its services mount by name,\nas a Compose file's volumes. Deleting one destroys its data, though Dockwell\nkeeps it for 7 days first. Docker can't change a volume, so its spec can't\nchange once it is made.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "driver", Help: "The volume driver: \"local\" unless set."},
				{Path: "options", Help: "The driver's options, such as type, device and o for an NFS mount. They may hold secrets, as a CIFS mount's password goes in o. The local driver takes type none with bind, nfs, nfs4, cifs or tmpfs.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "labels", Help: "Labels on the volume, for tools that read them."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "network",
			Noun:  "networks",
			Short: "A network in an environment, kind \"network\"",
			Long:  "Network is a Docker network in an environment, which its services join by name,\nas a Compose file's networks. A service reaches another on a network they share\nby the other's name. Docker can't change a network, so its spec can't change\nonce it is made.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "driver", Help: "The network driver: \"bridge\" unless set."},
				{Path: "options", Help: "The driver's options."},
				{Path: "internal", Help: "Cuts the network off from outside the server: only its containers reach each other."},
				{Path: "labels", Help: "Labels on the network, for tools that read them."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "variable",
			Noun:  "variables",
			Short: "A variable of an environment, kind \"variable\"",
			Long:  "Variable is a value of an environment that its services use, such as\nLOG_LEVEL=info. It isn't secret: plans, history and the panel show it. Keep\nanything that is in a Secret.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "value", Help: "The value."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "secret",
			Noun:  "secrets",
			Short: "A secret of an environment, kind \"secret\"",
			Long:  "Secret is a value of an environment that its services use without anyone reading\nit back, such as a database password. Dockwell keeps it encrypted, sends it only\nto the servers that run a service that uses it, and never shows it in plans,\nhistory or logs. Revealing it takes a passkey, and is audited.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "value", Help: "The value. Every edit sets it whole.", Sensitive: true},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "route",
			Noun:  "routes",
			Short: "A domain or path sent to a service, kind \"route\"",
			Long:  "Route sends a domain's traffic, or a path under it, to a port of a service in\nthe route's environment, or to a port on the server itself. It comes over HTTPS\nwith a certificate Dockwell gets for the domain, unless tls says TLS ends before\nthis server. A server's first route starts its ingress, which takes ports 80 and\n443, or port 80 alone while no route on the server takes a certificate; its last\nstops it and frees them. See docs/adr/0024-the-caddy-ingress-driver.md and\ndocs/adr/0030-routes-behind-a-tunnel-or-a-proxy.md.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "domain", Help: "The domain, such as \"app.example.com\": a lowercase DNS name that a public certificate authority issues for. Wildcards aren't taken."},
				{Path: "path", Help: "The path it covers, such as \"/api\": that path and what is under it, which reach the service as they are. Empty covers the whole domain."},
				{Path: "service", Help: "The service to send it to, by name, in the route's environment. With host_port it says whose route this is, as the route dials that port rather than the service's container. Empty only for a host_port that is no service's."},
				{Path: "port", Help: "The port the service listens on inside its container, such as 8080. Empty when host_port names a port on the server instead."},
				{Path: "tls", Help: "\"off\" when TLS ends before this server, as at a tunnel or a proxy in front of it: the domain is served over plain HTTP on port 80, with no certificate and no redirect to HTTPS, so its traffic reaches this server unencrypted. Such a route answers only the proxies in trusted_proxies, or with none, this server's own loopback, unless plain_for_anyone says otherwise. Anyone else gets what a name with no route gets. Empty gets a certificate. A domain's routes all take the same setting. A person approves taking a domain that has had a certificate off it."},
				{Path: "trusted_proxies", Help: "The proxies in front of this server whose word this route takes on where a request came from, each an address, a range such as \"203.0.113.0/24\", or \"cloudflare\" for Cloudflare's published ranges. A request from anywhere else has its address taken from the connection, whatever another route on the server names. From an address or a range, X-Forwarded-For is read from the right. From \"cloudflare\", CF-Connecting-IP is read, as Cloudflare documents, and it's only as sure as Cloudflare says: see docs/security/ingress.md. A person approves each one a change adds, as a proxy that isn't really one makes client addresses forgeable. A range of Cloudflare's is named by the preset, and a public range wider than /12, or /28 in IPv6, or a private one wider than /8, isn't taken."},
				{Path: "host_port", Help: "A port on the server itself, such as 8080, in place of port, for a service on the server's own network, which a route can't send to by name, or with no service at all, for something that isn't a Dockwell service. The proxy dials 127.0.0.1 on it, so what's there sees every caller as local. It reaches past containers, so a person approves it, and any change to where it answers. Dockwell's own ports, and Docker's API, on 2375, 2376 or wherever dockerd listens, aren't taken."},
				{Path: "plain_for_anyone", Help: "True for a route that says tls off to answer anyone who can reach this server's port 80, as for a site on a private network, or a load balancer whose addresses aren't known. It goes around whatever is in front, so a person approves it."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "backup_policy",
			Noun:  "backup-policies",
			Short: "A schedule of backups of a volume, kind \"backup.policy\"",
			Long:  "BackupPolicy backs up a volume in its environment on a schedule, on every server\nthat has it, and keeps the newest of those backups. A backup someone takes by\nhand is theirs, and no policy deletes it. The control plane runs the schedule,\nso a run due while it's down happens when it's back.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "volume", Help: "The volume, by name, in this environment."},
				{Path: "every", Help: "How often it runs."},
				{Path: "at", Help: "The time it runs, as 24-hour HH:MM: \"03:00\" unless set."},
				{Path: "weekday", Help: "The day it runs each week, as 0 for Sunday to 6 for Saturday."},
				{Path: "time_zone", Help: "The time zone at is in, such as \"Europe/Amsterdam\". Left out, a change that makes or updates the policy sets the install's time zone, from Settings, and UTC where the install has none."},
				{Path: "keep", Help: "How many of its backups to keep, on each server, newest first: 7 unless set."},
				{Path: "stop", Help: "Stop what runs on the volume for an exact copy of its files, and start it again after. A database's dump stops nothing. Without it, a copy of a running app's files can hold half a write."},
				{Path: "destination", Help: "The backup destination each backup also goes to, by name. Empty keeps them on the server alone, so they go if it does."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "backup_destination",
			Noun:  "backup-destinations",
			Short: "S3-compatible storage backups go to, kind \"backup.destination\"",
			Long:  "BackupDestination is S3-compatible storage that backups go to as well as the\nserver's disk, so they outlive it: Amazon S3, Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, MinIO\nand the like. It lives in the organization, and any environment's backups can\nuse it. A backup is encrypted on its server before it leaves, so the storage\nnever reads what it holds. Dockwell keeps this encrypted, and sends it only to\nthe server that's sending a backup, for that one call. See\ndocs/adr/0033-backups-of-volumes-and-databases.md.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "endpoint", Help: "The storage's address, such as \"https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com\"."},
				{Path: "region", Help: "The bucket's region: \"auto\" unless set."},
				{Path: "bucket", Help: "The bucket."},
				{Path: "prefix", Help: "What each object's name starts with, such as \"dockwell\". Empty uses the bucket from the top."},
				{Path: "access_key", Help: "The access key's ID.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "secret_key", Help: "The access key's secret.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "passphrase", Help: "What backups are encrypted with before they leave. Nothing but this opens them, and dockwell open-backup needs it: keep a copy somewhere that isn't this install. Set it when you add a destination, and leave it out of an update, which keeps it: an update that sets one is refused, as every copy there is sealed with it.", Sensitive: true},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "database_user",
			Noun:  "database-users",
			Short: "A database and the user who owns it, in a database server a service runs, kind \"database.user\"",
			Long:  "DatabaseUser is a database inside a database server that a service of its\nenvironment runs, with the user who owns it, who signs in with a secret of the\nenvironment. The service runs PostgreSQL, MySQL or MariaDB from its official\nimage, and the user and the database are made in its own container, with its own\ntools, on the servers it runs on (ADR 0038). Its name is the user's, such as\n\"shop\". Setting the secret anew changes the user's password, and deleting one\ndrops the user and the database, with everything in it.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "service", Help: "The service that runs the database server, by name, such as \"db\"."},
				{Path: "database_name", Help: "The database the user owns, which is made with them, such as \"shop\". Empty takes the user's name. It can't change once made."},
				{Path: "password", Help: "The environment's secret the user's password is in, by name, such as \"SHOP_DATABASE_PASSWORD\"."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "alert_rule",
			Noun:  "alert-rules",
			Short: "What Dockwell watches for, and who it tells, kind \"alert.rule\"",
			Long:  "AlertRule is something Dockwell watches for, on every server or app it applies\nto, and who it tells when it holds. It lives in the organization, and the\ncontrol plane evaluates it, from what it hears of servers already. See\ndocs/adr/0044-alerts.md.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "condition", Help: "What it watches for."},
				{Path: "threshold", Help: "The level, for a condition that has one: a percent for disk and memory, 1 to 100, and days for a certificate, 1 to 90. Others leave it 0."},
				{Path: "duration", Help: "How long the condition holds before the alert fires, such as \"5m\". Empty fires at once. For a late backup, how long after its time a run may start."},
				{Path: "severity", Help: "How much it matters: warning unless set."},
				{Path: "channels", Help: "The channels it sends to, by name. Empty sends to every channel, so a rule tells someone as soon as there is one."},
				{Path: "repeat", Help: "Sends an alert that's still firing again this often, such as \"4h\". Empty sends it once, and once more when it resolves."},
				{Path: "disabled", Help: "Keeps the rule without evaluating it."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "alert_channel",
			Noun:  "alert-channels",
			Short: "Where alerts are sent, kind \"alert.channel\"",
			Long:  "AlertChannel is somewhere alerts are sent. It lives in the organization. What\nsigns in to it is sealed, and a plan says only that it changed (ADR 0022).",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "webhook.url", Help: "The URL, such as \"https://hooks.example.com/dockwell\". It may hold a token, so it's sealed, and lists show only its host.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "webhook.secret", Help: "What signs each request, in a Dockwell-Signature header, so the endpoint can tell it came from this install. Empty signs nothing.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "email.host", Help: "The SMTP server's name or address, such as \"smtp.example.com\"."},
				{Path: "email.port", Help: "Its port: 587 for STARTTLS and 465 for TLS unless set, and 25 for none."},
				{Path: "email.security", Help: "How Dockwell secures the connection."},
				{Path: "email.username", Help: "Who Dockwell signs in as, when the server asks for that. Empty signs in as no one."},
				{Path: "email.password", Help: "The password Dockwell signs in with. Changing the server, its port, its security or the user needs it again.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "email.from", Help: "Who the emails come from, such as \"Dockwell <alerts@example.com>\"."},
				{Path: "email.to", Help: "Who they go to, such as \"ops@example.com\": one to ten addresses."},
				{Path: "ntfy.server", Help: "The server, such as \"https://ntfy.sh\", which it is unless set."},
				{Path: "ntfy.topic", Help: "The topic, such as \"dockwell-alerts-x7f2\". On a public server anyone who knows it can read what's sent, so it's sealed like a password, and lists show only the server.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "ntfy.token", Help: "An access token, for a server or topic that asks for one. Changing the server needs it again.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "slack.url", Help: "The webhook's URL, such as \"https://hooks.slack.com/services/T000/B000/XXXX\". It's what lets anyone post there, so it's sealed, and lists show only its host.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "discord.url", Help: "The webhook's URL, such as \"https://discord.com/api/webhooks/0000/XXXX\". It's what lets anyone post there, so it's sealed, and lists show only its host.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "telegram.bot_token", Help: "The bot's token, such as \"123456789:AAE…\", which BotFather gives.", Sensitive: true},
				{Path: "telegram.chat_id", Help: "The chat, by its ID, such as \"-1001234567890\" for a group, or a channel's name, such as \"@example_alerts\". The bot has to be in it."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "alert_check",
			Noun:  "alert-checks",
			Short: "An address checked for whether it answers, kind \"alert.check\" (ADR 0066)",
			Long:  "AlertCheck is an address Dockwell asks, every so often, whether it answers as it\nshould: an uptime check. It lives in the organization, and the control plane\nruns it. A run is one GET, with no redirect followed, that keeps nothing of the\nanswer but its status, how long it took, and whether the keyword was there. See\ndocs/adr/0066-checks-of-a-url.md.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "address", Help: "The address it asks, an http or https URL, such as \"https://shop.example.com/\"."},
				{Path: "every", Help: "How often it's checked: \"30s\", \"1m\", \"5m\" or \"15m\". \"1m\" unless set."},
				{Path: "timeout", Help: "How long a run waits for an answer: \"10s\" unless set, at most \"30s\", and less than every."},
				{Path: "down_after", Help: "How many runs in a row have to fail for it to read down: 2 unless set, 1 to 10. Down, it reads up again after 2 runs in a row pass."},
				{Path: "expect_from", Help: "The lowest status that counts as up: 200 unless set."},
				{Path: "expect_to", Help: "The highest status that counts as up: 399 unless set, so a redirect is up."},
				{Path: "keyword", Help: "Text the answer's first 64 KiB has to hold, when set, such as \"Welcome\"."},
				{Path: "keyword_absent", Help: "Says the keyword has to be absent instead, such as \"Error\"."},
				{Path: "inside_network", Help: "Lets the check reach the private networks the control server is on, such as an address only the office's network serves. Unset, it reaches the internet alone, keyword included, and a name that resolves to a private address reads failing with how to allow it. Never this server's loopback, nor a metadata or link-local address. Plans, the Checks tab and Activity show it (ADR 0066)."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "catalog_source",
			Noun:  "catalog-sources",
			Short: "A catalog read from a Git repository, kind \"catalog.source\"",
			Long:  "CatalogSource is a catalog Dockwell reads apps from, beside its own: a folder of\nentries in its format in a public Git repository, at one commit. Moving it to\nanother commit is a change, so what anyone can install from it moves only with a\nplan. See docs/adr/0055-catalogs-from-a-git-url.md.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "repository", Help: "The repository's URL, over HTTPS, such as \"https://github.com/example/catalog\"."},
				{Path: "path", Help: "The folder of entries in it, one <id>.yaml each, such as \"entries\". Empty is its top."},
				{Path: "commit", Help: "The commit it's read at, in full, as 40 hex digits."},
			},
		},
		{
			Field: "task",
			Noun:  "tasks",
			Short: "Work that runs on a schedule, kind \"task\"",
			Long:  "Task is work that runs on a schedule, on each server it's placed on, kind \"task\"\n(ADR 0053): a shell command on the server, a command in an app's image, or an\nHTTP request. Each server's agent keeps its time, so a task runs while the\ncontrol plane is away.",
			Fields: []apicli.Field{
				{Path: "shell.command", Help: "The command, run with /bin/sh -c, such as \"find /var/log/app -mtime +7 -delete\"."},
				{Path: "shell.working_dir", Help: "The folder it runs in: / unless set."},
				{Path: "app.service", Help: "The app, by name, in the task's environment."},
				{Path: "app.command", Help: "The command, as words split as a shell splits them, run after the app's entrypoint in place of its command, such as \"/app/api report sales\". It runs without a shell unless it names one."},
				{Path: "request.url", Help: "The URL, http or https."},
				{Path: "request.method", Help: "GET unless set, or HEAD or POST, with no body."},
				{Path: "request.expect_status", Help: "The status it expects, such as 204. Any 2xx unless set."},
				{Path: "schedule", Help: "When it runs, in cron's five fields, minute, hour, day of the month, month and day of the week, such as \"0 4 * * *\", or @hourly, @daily, @weekly, @monthly or @yearly. Nothing runs more often than once a minute."},
				{Path: "time_zone", Help: "The time zone schedule is in, such as \"Europe/Amsterdam\". Left out, a change that makes or updates the task sets the install's time zone, from Settings, and UTC where the install has none."},
				{Path: "timeout_seconds", Help: "How long a run may take, in seconds, before it's stopped: 600 unless set, at most 86400."},
				{Path: "retries", Help: "How many more times a run that fails or times out tries, a minute apart: 0 to 3."},
				{Path: "paused", Help: "Keeps it from running on its schedule. Run now still runs it."},
				{Path: "skip_missed", Help: "Skips a run missed while its server was off or its agent stopped, rather than running it once when the agent starts."},
				{Path: "display_name", Help: "What people call it, such as \"Nightly sales report\". Its name when empty."},
			},
		},
	}
}
